NB: Please ignore if my comment is orthogonal to the thread.

Disabling my provider block removed the check indicator.

```
"provider": {
    "openai":
         /** redacted *//
    }
}
```

To get the auth menu to restore and allow non-API Key, I had to disable the plugin `opencode-websearch-cited`.  This plugin depends on the openai provider config, so there being an error makes sense. 

@rekram1-node closing this out, but probably room for auth resiliency here, these were unexpected side-effects.

The fix is:

```
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "keybinds": {
    "input_paste": "alt+v"
  }
}
```

in your opencode config

Thanks to dansan92: https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/issues/1429#issuecomment-3883607598

Interested in implementing this. My use case is time-of-day routing to avoid provider degradation under peak load. The other use cases in this thread are compelling too — cost routing, fallback chains, per-tool selection, A/B testing — and this seems to cover most if not all of them.

### Why not `chat.params`?

Provider, language model, and auth are resolved before `chat.params` runs (`llm.ts:97-101`). A `chat.model` hook before resolution avoids re-resolving and breaking changes.

### Proposed signature

```typescript
"chat.model"?: (
  input: {
    sessionID: string
    agent: string
    model: Model
    provider: ProviderContext
    message: UserMessage
  },
  output: {
    model: { providerID: string; modelID: string }
    persistent: boolean  // true = switch for session, false = this call only
  }
) => Promise<void>
```

Follows existing pattern — caller pre-populates defaults, plugin mutates what it needs:

```typescript
// per-call routing
"chat.model": async (input, output) => {
  if (isPeakHours()) output.model = { providerID: "openai", modelID: "gpt-4o" }
}

// persistent fallback
"chat.model": async (input, output) => {
  if (this.failures > 3) {
    output.model = { providerID: "anthropic", modelID: "claude-sonnet-4-20250514" }
    output.persistent = true
  }
}
```


My only open question would be if the UI indicate when a plugin routes to a different model?

For Linux/WSL users, you can manually unarchive sessions via sqlite3:

Open DB: sqlite3 ~/.local/share/opencode/opencode.db
Find your session ID: SELECT id, title FROM session WHERE time_archived > 0;
Set it to 0: UPDATE session SET time_archived = 0 WHERE id = 'your_id';

Then restart/reload your client. Done.
